.\"
.TH "gfx.toml" "8" "@PACKAGE_VERSION@" "xrdp team" ""
.SH "NAME"
\fBgfx.toml\fR \- Configuration file for xrdp(8) graphics pipeline extension

.SH "DESCRIPTION"

This file configures the detailed settings of the Graphics Pipeline Extension
for xrdp(8). The file format is TOML. See the link in the SEE ALSO section for
TOML syntax.

The file contains following sections:

.TP
\fB[codec]\fR \- configure preferred codec and encoders for \fBxrdp\fR(8).

.TP
\fB[x264]\fR \- parameters for x264 encoder.

.TP
\fB[OpenH264]\fR \- parameters for Cisco OpenH264 encoder.

.LP
All options and values are case-sensitive, with some exception, and are
described in detail below.

.SH "CODEC"
\fB[codec]\fR section defines preferred codec order and encoder. The options
to be specified in this section are the following:

.TP
\fBorder\fR = \fI<array>\fR
Define the order in which codecs should be used, "H.264" and "RFX".
Specify as an array of strings like \fB[ "H.264", "RFX ]\fR.
Strings in the array are case-insensitive, while others are case-sensitive.

.TP
\fBh264_encoder\fR = \fI<string>\fR
Specify a preferred H.264 encoder, \fB"x264"\fR or \fB"OpenH264"\fR.
This parameter takes effect only when more than one encoder is
enabled at compile time. If only one H.264 encoder is enabled, the encoder
will be used regardless the value of this parameter. Defaults to \fB"x264"\fR
if not specified or if an invalid encoder is specified. The encoder name is
case-insensitive.

.SH "X264"
\fB[x264]\fR section defines encoding parameters that will be passed to
x264 encoder. See \fBx264 --fullhelp\fR for the detailed explanations of the
parameters. The options to be specified in this section are following:

.TP
\fBpreset\fR = \fI<string>\fR
Select a preset encoding settings. Slower presets result in higher CPU usage
but offer better screen image quality and require lower network bandwidth.
Here are available presets:

.B ultrafast, superfast, veryfast, faster, fast, medium, slow, slower,
veryslow, placebo

Presets slower than \fBmedium\fR may not suitable for use with xrdp.

.TP
\fBtune\fR = \fI<string>\fR
Select a tune for source or situation. \fBzerolatency\fR is most appropriate
for use with xrdp. Here are available options:

.B film, grain, stillimage, psnr, ssim, fastdecode, zerolatency

.TP
\fBprofile\fR = \fI<string>\fR
Select a profile. Here are available options:

.B main, baseline, high, high10, high422, high444

.TP
\fBvbv_max_bitrate\fR = \fI<integer>\fR
Set the maximum fill rate for the VBV (Video Buffering Vefifier) buffer
in kbps.

.TP
\fBvbv_buffer_size\fR = \fI<integer>\fR
Set the size of the VBV buffer size in kilobits.

.TP
\fBfps_num\fR = \fI<integer>\fR
Set the fps numerator.

.TP
\fBfps_den\fR = \fI<integer>\fR
Set the fps denominator.

.TP
\fBthreads = \fI<integer>\fR
Specify how many CPU threads to use for H.264 encoding per screen (0 for
auto). Carefully evaluate this value when configuring it to avoid exhausting
the available threads. For example, if 3 users are connected simultaneously
with dual screens, xrdp may use up to <threads> * 3 * 2 threads for H.264
encoding. Please also note that too many threads can hurt quality.

.SH "OPENH264"

\fB[OpenH264]\fR section defines encoding parameters that will be passed to
OpenH264 encoder. The options to be specified in this section are following:

.TP
\fBEnableFrameSkip\fR = \fI<boolean>\fR
Allows the encoder to skip frames in order to keep the bitrate within the
limits if it is about to exceed the maximum bitrate set by MaxBitrate.

.TP
\fBTargetBitrate\fR = \fI<integer>\fR
Sets the target average bitrate (in bps) that the encoder will attempt to
achieve throughout the encoding process.

.TP
\fBMaxBitrate\fR = \fI<integer>\fR
Sets an upper limit of the bitrate in bps.
.TP
\fBMaxFrameRate\fR = \fI<float>\fR
Sets the maximum frame rate that the encoder will process per second.

.SH "AVC444_FFMPEG"

\fB[avc444_ffmpeg]\fR configures the external stock-\fBffmpeg\fR AVC444 backend,
used when \fBh264_encoder = "ffmpeg"\fR is selected in \fB[codec]\fR. xrdp spawns
one persistent \fBffmpeg\fR child per surface (via \fBfork\fR/\fBexecve\fR \(em
no shell is involved) and streams raw NV12 into it, muxing both AVC444 views
(main YUV420 and the Chroma420 auxiliary) into a single H.264 stream.

xrdp owns only two fixed contracts of the \fBffmpeg\fR command line and does not
interpret anything in between:

.TP
.B Input contract (fixed)
Raw NV12 on \fBpipe:3\fR at the 16\(hyaligned coded size, BT.709 full range,
fed by the AVC444 converter. Not configurable.

.TP
.B Output contract (fixed)
Annex\(hyB H.264 wrapped in NUT on \fBpipe:1\fR
(\fB\-bsf:v h264_mp4toannexb \-f nut pipe:1\fR), which the in\(hytree NUT demux
and H.264 parser require. Not configurable.

.PP
Everything between those contracts \(em the codec selection (\fB\-c:v\fR) and
all of its tuning \(em is supplied verbatim by the administrator and passed
straight through to \fBexecve\fR. This is a deliberate design choice: xrdp does
\fBnot\fR enumerate individual encoder flags, so arbitrary tuning and hardware
encoders (\fBh264_nvenc\fR, \fBh264_qsv\fR, \fBh264_vaapi\fR, and so on) can be
expressed without changing xrdp.

.PP
The AVC444 version is negotiated automatically per client and needs no
configuration. When the client advertises AVC444 v2 support (MS-RDPEGFX
capability version 10.1 or later) xrdp emits AVC444 v2 (codec id 0x000F,
ChromaV2), which avoids the magenta/purple chroma fringing that AVC444 v1
produces on saturated colored text and edges; clients that advertise only v1
(capability version 10.0) transparently receive AVC444 v1 (codec id 0x000E).
Both versions feed the same two NV12 pictures per frame to \fBffmpeg\fR, so the
\fBencoder_args\fR below apply unchanged to either version.

.TP
\fBpath\fR = \fI<string>\fR
Absolute path to the \fBffmpeg\fR binary. Default: \fB/usr/bin/ffmpeg\fR.

.TP
\fBchroma_align\fR = \fI<integer>\fR
Coded\(hywidth alignment for the AVC444 v2 chroma (ChromaV2) auxiliary view,
either \fB16\fR or \fB32\fR. Default: \fB32\fR.

The auxiliary view packs its U and V halves split at \fIcoded_width / 2\fR.
Clients derive that split from the surface width but with different rounding:
Microsoft \fBmstsc\fR uses a 32\(hyaligned width, while \fBFreeRDP\fR uses a
16\(hyaligned width. When the two differ \(em i.e. the 16\(hyaligned width is
not already 32\(hyaligned (an odd macroblock count) \(em a client using the
other rounding reads the V samples 16 pixels displaced, producing a period\(hy2
chroma "comb" left of high\(hycontrast vertical edges plus a variable
right\(hyedge strip, on roughly half of window widths.

Set \fB32\fR (the default) for \fBmstsc\fR / Windows Remote Desktop clients; set
\fB16\fR for \fBFreeRDP\fR\(hybased clients. A deployment cannot satisfy both
alignments at a non\(hy32\(hyaligned width simultaneously.

.TP
\fBavc_mode\fR = \fI<string>\fR
Selects AVC444 versus plain AVC420 (codec id \fB0x000B\fR, a single YUV420 view)
over the same \fBffmpeg\fR backend. One of \fB"auto"\fR, \fB"444"\fR or
\fB"420"\fR. Default: \fB"auto"\fR.

.RS
.IP \(bu 3
\fB"auto"\fR \(em serve AVC444 to clients that advertise it, otherwise AVC420.
This is the recommended setting.
.IP \(bu 3
\fB"444"\fR \(em serve AVC444 only; a client that advertises solely AVC420 gets
no H.264 (the next configured codec, if any, is used).
.IP \(bu 3
\fB"420"\fR \(em force AVC420 for any H.264\(hycapable client. Because the RDP
client, not the server, has no user\(hyfacing 420/444 switch (it advertises a
capability set and the server elects the codec), this is how a fixed client such
as \fBmstsc\fR \(em which always advertises AVC444 \(em can be exercised on the
AVC420 path. Takes effect on the next connection; no restart is required.
.RE

AVC420 halves chroma resolution versus AVC444, so saturated colored text and
edges look softer; it is provided mainly for compatibility and A/B comparison.
The same \fBencoder_args\fR (including hardware encoders) apply to both.

.TP
\fBdump_extra\fR = \fI<boolean>\fR
Static in\(hyband parameter\(hyset policy; set it to match the encoder chosen
in \fBencoder_args\fR. Default: \fBfalse\fR.

.RS
.IP \(bu 3
\fBfalse\fR \(em the encoder itself repeats SPS/PPS in\(hyband on every
keyframe (\fBlibx264\fR with \fBrepeat\-headers=1\fR, \fBh264_vaapi\fR).
.IP \(bu 3
\fBtrue\fR \(em the encoder emits parameter sets only as global extradata
(\fBh264_nvenc\fR); the \fBdump_extra\fR bitstream filter reinserts them into
the byte stream.
.RE

The RDP GFX wire carries raw Annex\(hyB NAL units with no extradata
side\(hychannel, so the parameter sets must be in the byte stream exactly once:
a missing SPS/PPS is undecodable everywhere, and a duplicated SPS/PPS blacks
out strict decoders (the macOS Windows App). The pre\(hyconnect verification
therefore refuses the AVC candidate loudly \(em with the failure class in the
log \(em when the declared value does not match the encoder's real bitstream.
The value is never adapted at runtime.

.TP
\fBltr_rekey_frame_num\fR = \fI<integer>\fR
Applies only when \fBaux_ltr_chain\fR is enabled. The shared \fBframe_num\fR
value at which the encoder pair is rebuilt and the client's decoder is reset.
Default: \fB65024\fR (2^16\(hy512).

The AVC444 long\(hyterm\(hyreference chain drives one shared, monotonically
increasing \fBframe_num\fR across both views. A decoder that reaches the
16\(hybit wrap in this topology has been measured to stop decoding silently, so
the server re\(hykeys before the counter can get there: it destroys the encoder
pair, so the replacement opens with a fresh IDR and the shared counter resets,
and repaints the whole surface in the same frame. The visible cost is one
full\(hyframe update. See \fBltr_rekey_surface_reset\fR for whether the re\(hykey
also emits an EGFX surface teardown.

At the default the boundary arrives after roughly 18 minutes of continuous
30 fps animation; frames are encoded only on damage, so an idle session may
never reach it. Lower the value to exercise the boundary deliberately (a test
arm at \fB536\fR crosses it every few seconds). Accepted range is \fB64\fR to
\fB65024\fR; a value outside it is refused with a warning and the default is
kept, because a higher threshold would expose a decoder to the very wrap this
mechanism exists to prevent.

.TP
\fBltr_rekey_surface_reset\fR = \fItrue\fR | \fIfalse\fR
Applies only when \fBaux_ltr_chain\fR is enabled. Whether the re\(hykey also
deletes and recreates the affected EGFX surface, in addition to rebuilding the
encoder pair. Default: \fBfalse\fR.

The surface teardown is a protocol\(hydefined decoder reset, and was intended as
belt\(hyand\(hybraces on top of the fresh IDR. It is not what the re\(hykey is for:
the counter reset comes from the encoder rebuild alone. Clients that repaint
the output when a mapped surface is replaced show a black flash at every
boundary \(em observed on macOS, and observed to stop when the teardown is
masked \(em so the default keeps the re\(hykey entirely inside the bitstream and
the client sees only a full\(hysurface repaint from a fresh IDR. Set this to
\fBtrue\fR only to reproduce the old behaviour deliberately.

.TP
\fBeager_slot_ack\fR = \fItrue\fR | \fIfalse\fR
Applies only when \fBaux_ltr_chain\fR is enabled, and requires a matching
xorgxrdp. Default: \fBtrue\fR. Set it to \fBfalse\fR to restore the
acknowledgement behaviour shipped before this release.

The X server may capture a new frame only into a buffer the encoder has
finished reading, and it learns that a buffer is free from an acknowledgement
the server sends. The older rule sent that acknowledgement when the frame's
last byte had been handed to the network, which is several steps after the
encoder actually finished with the buffer \(em so the number it carried could
never refer to a frame still inside the encoder, whatever the window was set
to, and capture, encode and transmission ran one after another instead of
overlapping. With this enabled the acknowledgement is sent as soon as the
encoder has drained the buffer, and how far ahead the server may run is
\fBwire_window\fR below.

The shipped \fBwire_window\fR of \fB1\fR reproduces the older bound on client
frames outstanding. An installation that upgrades and changes no
configuration therefore puts no more frames on the wire than it did before.
Setting \fB2\fR permits one additional client frame; it may improve overlap,
but increases queued memory and input-to-display latency by that inventory.

.TP
\fBwire_window\fR = \fI<integer>\fR
How far the client is allowed to fall behind, in frames, before the server
stops capturing new ones. Default: \fB1\fR, which is what the mechanism this
replaces allowed; see \fBeager_slot_ack\fR above for what \fB2\fR costs.
Accepted range is \fB1\fR to \fB64\fR; a
value outside it is refused with a warning and the default is kept. Applies
only when \fBeager_slot_ack\fR is enabled; the legacy path is unaffected.

.SS What it counts, and why that matters with more than one monitor
Read this part before setting the value on a multi\(hymonitor session; the
number does not mean what it appears to mean there.

A "frame" here is \fBone monitor's frame, not one refresh of the desktop\fR.
The server numbers each captured image with a single counter that advances
once per monitor, so a desktop spanning M monitors consumes M of these
numbers every time it refreshes.

\fBwire_window\fR is counted in those numbers and is a single value for the
whole session \(em it is not per monitor. The capture slots underneath it
\fIare\fR per monitor. So at the instant a frame is handed to the transport,
the number of frames the client has not yet acknowledged is at most

.RS
.nf
wire_window + 2 x (number of monitors)
.fi
.RE

\(em with the default and two monitors, at most 5; with
\fBwire_window\fR = \fB2\fR and two monitors, at most 6. The two per monitor are
the capture slots the X server may fill ahead of the acknowledgement it is
waiting on, and they are fixed: they are a property of the shared memory the
two halves of the server exchange, not a setting.

The consequence for an administrator is the part worth remembering:
\fBadding a monitor divides the window's headroom between the screens.\fR
A value of 2 gives a single screen two frames of headroom, two screens about
one each, and three screens less than one each. Nothing in the configuration
changes and nothing is logged; the same number simply means less per
screen.

This asymmetry is not a design goal, it is what the code does today, and it
is documented here rather than hidden: the window predates multi\(hymonitor
support on this path and counts a quantity that has since become
per\(hymonitor.

.SS Choosing a value
This is the ONE end\(hyto\(hyend limit on how much video may be in flight
between the server and the display, and it is the only knob here whose right
value the server cannot work out for itself \(em it follows from the network,
not from the machine. On a link that acknowledges a frame faster than the
server can produce one, the window is never the limit and the default is
fine.

On a long link it is the limit, and the frame rate cannot exceed the whole
bound above per round trip \(em not \fBwire_window\fR alone:

.RS
.nf
frames per second <= (wire_window + 2 x monitors) / round trip
.fi
.RE

At 80 ms round trip the default of 1 gives about 37 frames per second on one
monitor and about 62 across two \(em but that is the total across all
screens, so it is still about 31 per screen. At 200 ms, about 15 on one
monitor and 25 across two. Setting it to 2 raises those to about 50 and 75
at 80 ms, and 20 and 30 at 200 ms.

Raising it buys frame rate at the cost of latency: every extra frame in
flight is one more frame of delay between an action and seeing it, and one
more frame of memory queued in the server. At 4K that is roughly 3.3 MB per
unit. Raise it only as far as the round trip demands.

\fBOn a multi\(hymonitor session, do not expect raising it to cure
stuttering.\fR It was measured on two monitors: doubling the window made
long waits rarer but left the typical wait unchanged, and did not change the
frame rate. On a fast link the limit on a multi\(hymonitor session is the
server encoding each screen in turn, not this window, and no value here
addresses that.

When the window is reached the server does not queue the frames it did not
send; it does not capture them at all, and the damage they would have carried
is merged into the next frame it does capture. A slow link therefore gets
fewer frames, each of them current, rather than a backlog of stale ones.

.TP
\fBencoder_args\fR = \fI<array of strings>\fR
The encoder block, inserted verbatim into the \fBffmpeg\fR argv immediately
after the input mapping and before the output framing. Each array element
becomes exactly one \fBexecve\fR argv token; because there is no shell, there is
no quoting, splitting or variable expansion \(em put each flag and each value in
its own element (e.g. \fB"\-crf", "16"\fR, not \fB"\-crf 16"\fR). Up to 64
tokens of 255 characters each are accepted; excess tokens are dropped with a
warning. An omitted key, or an explicitly empty array, selects the built\(hyin
default block.

The chosen encoder \fBmust\fR emit decodable Annex\(hyB H.264. For \fBlibx264\fR
this means including \fBrepeat\-headers=1\fR in \fB\-x264\-params\fR so every IDR
carries SPS/PPS; otherwise a client that joins the stream cannot decode it.

Because \fBgfx.toml\fR is root\(hyowned server configuration (trusted like
\fBsshd_config\fR), these tokens are not treated as untrusted input.

The built\(hyin default is tuned for interactive RDP:

.nf
encoder_args = [
  "\-c:v", "libx264",
  "\-bf", "0",
  "\-preset", "ultrafast",
  "\-tune", "zerolatency",
  "\-crf", "18",
  "\-g", "240",
  "\-x264\-params", "repeat\-headers=1",
]
.fi

\fB\-tune zerolatency\fR disables x264 lookahead, B\(hyframe reordering and
threaded\(hyframe delay so the child emits one encoded picture per input frame
instead of withholding several until end\(hyof\(hystream.

Example selecting an NVIDIA hardware encoder (xrdp needs no change to support
this; tested on a Tesla T4, driver 580.159.03, Ubuntu ffmpeg 8.0.1).
\fBh264_nvenc\fR keeps SPS/PPS in global extradata only, so
\fBdump_extra = true\fR is required:

.nf
[avc444_ffmpeg]
path = "/usr/bin/ffmpeg"
dump_extra = true
encoder_args = [
  "\-c:v", "h264_nvenc",
  "\-preset", "p1",
  "\-tune", "ll",
  "\-rc", "constqp",
  "\-qp", "20",
  "\-bf", "0",
  "\-delay", "0",
  "\-g", "240",
]
.fi

.PP
Example selecting a VAAPI hardware encoder (Intel/AMD render node). The AVC444
views arrive as system\(hymemory NV12, so they are uploaded to the GPU with
\fB\-vf format=nv12,hwupload\fR before \fBh264_vaapi\fR; \fB\-bf 0
\-async_depth 1\fR keep one encoded picture per input frame. Use
\fB\-vaapi_device\fR (which is accepted after \fB\-i\fR) rather than the global
\fB\-hwaccel/\-init_hw_device\fR forms, since \fBencoder_args\fR are inserted
after the input mapping:

.nf
[avc444_ffmpeg]
path = "/usr/bin/ffmpeg"
encoder_args = [
  "\-vaapi_device", "/dev/dri/renderD128",
  "\-vf", "format=nv12,hwupload",
  "\-c:v", "h264_vaapi",
  "\-rc_mode", "CQP",
  "\-qp", "20",
  "\-bf", "0",
  "\-async_depth", "1",
]
.fi

.PP
The \fBffmpeg\fR child is spawned by the xrdp connection process, which in a
standard install runs as \fBroot\fR, so it can open the render node
(\fB/dev/dri/renderD128\fR, typically \fBroot:root 0660\fR) with no extra
setup. If a deployment hardens xrdp to run as a non\(hyroot service user, add
that user to the render node's group (for example \fBchgrp render
/dev/dri/renderD128\fR and add the user to the \fBrender\fR group). The child is
started with a minimal environment (\fBPATH=/usr/bin:/bin\fR only); libva
normally autodetects the driver from the render node, but a GPU that needs an
explicit \fBLIBVA_DRIVER_NAME\fR cannot currently be steered through
\fBgfx.toml\fR.

.SH "PERFORMANCE TRACE"

Performance trace is available only in a build configured with
\fB\-\-enable-perf-trace\fR. It is absent from a default build. Set
\fBXRDP_PERF_TRACE=\fR\fIprefix\fR in xrdp's environment to arm the sink.
\fBXRDP_GFX_TRACE=1\fR selects graphics events and
\fBXRDP_ACK_TRACE=1\fR selects credit-frontier events. A selector without an
armed sink records nothing and produces one warning; per-frame events never
fall back to the normal log.

The connection process exclusively creates \fIprefix.pid\fR as mode 0600,
without following symlinks. The prefix directory must be owned by the
administrator and writable by xrdp's post-drop runtime user. Records are
newline-terminated \fBschema=1\fR key/value text. They contain monotonic time,
process and thread identity, event name, frame/monitor identities and bounded
integer metadata; they contain no captured pixels or input events. A separate
sink thread performs file I/O and final drain. Open, write, drain or close
failure is reported once at human rate.

No diagnostic timestamp crosses the xup connection from xorgxrdp. Capture
characterization uses xrdp's receive, encode and egress endpoints. The normal
forking configuration creates one trace lifecycle and file per connection.
With \fBfork=false\fR simultaneous sessions share one process and one file;
use that mode for characterization only with one active session.

.SH "CONNECTION TYPES"

\fB[x264]\fR and \fB[OpenH264]\fR section are tables (also known as
dictionaries) that have subtables with connection types in their keys.
For example, \fB[x264.lan]\fR, \fB[OpenH264.wan]\fR.

You can configure different parameters such as bitrate for the encoder
per connection type. Define the default parameter set first, this will be
inherited to all connection types unless explicitly overridden in each
connection type.

List of available connection types are:

.B lan, wan, broadband_high, satellite, broadband_low, modem, and default

Currently, xrdp does not support connection type autodetection. If autodetect
is selected on the client side, it will be treated as if LAN is selected.

.SH "EXAMPLES"
This is an example \fBgfx.toml\fR:

.nf
[codec]
order = [ "H.264", "RFX" ]
h264_encoder = "x264"

[x264.default]
preset = "ultrafast"
tune = "zerolatency"
profile = "main"     # profile is forced to baseline if preset == ultrafast
vbv_max_bitrate = 0
vbv_buffer_size = 0
fps_num = 60
fps_den = 1

[x264.lan]
# inherits default, everything is same with the default
[x264.wan]
# parameters that are not explicitly overridden inherit the default values
preset = "veryfast"
vbv_max_bitrate = 15_000
vbv_buffer_size = 1_500
.fi

There are multiple ways to represent the data structure in TOML format. The
following two representations are semantically equivalent but the latter is 
discouraged due to concerns about complexity and readability.

.nf
[x264.default]
preset = "ultrafast"
tune = "zerolatency"
.fi

.nf
x264 = { default = { preset = "ultrafast", tune="zerolatency" } }
.fi

.SH "SEE ALSO"

For more information on \fBgfx.toml\fR configuration, see the wiki page.

.UR https://github.com/neutrinolabs/xrdp/wiki/H.264-encoding
.UE

The syntax for TOML files can be found at the following page.

.UR https://toml.io/en/v1.0.0
.UE
